{t:"hayek",q:"In 'Planning and democracy', Hayek bases the 'philosophy of individualism' on…",o:["the assumption that people are egoistic and should be","the fact that no mind can include more than a sector of society's needs in its scale of values","the natural right to property","the proven efficiency of spontaneous market order"],a:1,e:"Hayek explicitly denies the egoism reading (p. 44); individualism rests on the limits of imagination and the partial nature of all value scales."},
{t:"hayek",q:"Which statement is consistent with Hayek's chapter?",o:["Democracy is the highest political end","Delegation of law-making power is objectionable in itself","It is the price of democracy that in some fields things must be left to chance","Dictatorship must inevitably extirpate freedom"],a:2,e:"p. 51. He calls democracy a means (p. 52), accepts delegation of general rule-making (p. 49), and says his point is not that dictatorship must destroy freedom (p. 52)."},
{t:"hayek",q:"Mannheim argued that sovereignty can be boundlessly strengthened without renouncing democratic control. Hayek replies that…",o:["parliament can control only where it has agreed on the aim; otherwise it ends up choosing persons with practically absolute power","Mannheim is right as long as parliament keeps the right to vote","experts are always democratically accountable","planning is compatible with democracy if done locally"],a:0,e:"p. 51: without agreement on ends, parliament can only accept or reject the whole plan; the system tends towards plebiscitarian dictatorship."},
{t:"hayek",q:"Which concept on the HC4 slides does NOT appear in 'Planning and democracy'?",o:["A complete ethical code","Delegation to experts","Plebiscitarian dictatorship","Spontaneous order"],a:3,e:"Spontaneous order comes from Hayek's later work (kosmos/taxis, via Moroni); the other three are central to ch. 5."},
{t:"hayek",q:"How does Hayek explain Hitler's rise to power in this chapter?",o:["Hitler destroyed a functioning democracy by force","Democracy had already decayed under demands for planning; Hitler seemed the only man strong enough to get things done","The working class organised a revolution","Laissez-faire capitalism produced a crisis that required a dictator"],a:1,e:"'Hitler did not have to destroy democracy; he merely took advantage of the decay of democracy' (p. 50)."},
{t:"needham",q:"According to Needham, why is a person's interest in clean air protected by public law rather than by a property right?",o:["Clean air has no economic value","Effects are diffuse, cause and effect are unclear, and hundreds of victims could hardly cooperate to sue","The ECHR forbids property rights in air","It has been decided that clean air matters less than the factory's interest"],a:1,e:"p. 28: the 'technical' reason. The door-colour example is the 'political' reason (option D)."},
{t:"needham",q:"What does Needham conclude about creating markets in property rights?",o:["They should replace land-use planning","They are infeasible in all cases","They are a feasible alternative only under certain limited circumstances, and feasibility is not desirability","They are always more efficient than regulation"],a:2,e:"p. 34. He explicitly does not recommend replacing planning (p. 23) and does not compare desirability."},
{t:"needham",q:"In Needham's terms, which is an example of the 'private law' way of protecting interests in land?",o:["A municipality refusing a building permit","Emission limits for factories","A homeowner taking her neighbour to court for undermining her foundations (right of support)","A zoning plan for a region"],a:2,e:"Private law: the holder of the right enforces it herself in court (pp. 27–28). The others are public-law measures."},
{t:"needham",q:"Why, according to Needham, do Dutch industrial estates tend to deteriorate?",o:["Municipalities refuse environmental permits","Firms cannot be obliged to join a maintenance association and leases are not tradable, so nobody holds the rights needed to maintain the estate","Property investors own too many estates","Dutch planning is too market-led"],a:1,e:"p. 32: the fix he suggests is making it possible to sell unexpired leases, i.e. changing a property right."},
{t:"needham",q:"How does Needham position his investigation relative to libertarian critics of planning (e.g. Sorensen)?",o:["He agrees that there are no external effects of land use","He accepts planning's goals and the legitimacy of the state influencing land use, and only questions the instruments","He argues that public authorities cannot protect anyone's interests","He argues that property rights are natural and absolute"],a:1,e:"p. 30: libertarians deny externalities or the state's capacity; Needham asks only whether goals can be reached by changing rights."},
{t:"olson",q:"Hindmoor argues that the common reading of Olson as showing that collective action is impossible or irrational is…",o:["correct: Olson proves groups cannot organise","wrong: Olson argues groups face a collective action problem but may overcome it","correct only for small groups","irrelevant, because Olson used game theory to prove the opposite"],a:1,e:"pp. 102, 113 and 127. Olson did not use game theory himself (Box 5.2)."},
{t:"olson",q:"Two farmers' crops depend on a dam that is maintained only if BOTH work on it. Which game is this and what follows?",o:["Prisoners' dilemma: both defect","Chicken: each waits for the other","Assurance game: no dominant strategy, and cooperation should be easily achieved","Tragedy of the commons: a leviathan is needed"],a:2,e:"pp. 110–111. If one day's work by EITHER farmer suffices, it becomes chicken."},
{t:"olson",q:"The 'second-order collective action problem' refers to…",o:["the fact that punishing or rewarding contributors (selective incentives) is itself a collective good that someone must provide","a collective action problem that occurs twice in a row","conflict between two different groups","the failure of the state to coerce"],a:0,e:"pp. 116–117: why would anyone ostracise the one who failed to ostracise? Entrepreneurs or process benefits can break the regress."},
{t:"olson",q:"What does Hindmoor conclude about the relationship between group size and latency?",o:["Large groups can never organise","Small groups are by definition privileged","It is at most an empirical tendency: what matters is whether members can see who cooperates","Group size has no relationship at all with latency"],a:2,e:"p. 121: a large mining community may know its free-riders; bed-sit residents may not."},
{t:"olson",q:"Ostrom's work on common-pool resources, as presented by Hindmoor, shows that…",o:["only privatisation saves the commons","only a coercive leviathan saves the commons","communities can devise their own rules of allocation, monitoring and sanctioning, sustaining cooperation without a leviathan or the market","commons always end in tragedy"],a:2,e:"p. 120: conditional (tit-for-tat) cooperation, most likely with long-term interaction, costly exit, cheap monitoring and integrated groups."},
{t:"olson",q:"Which is a NEGATIVE selective incentive in Olson's sense?",o:["Free legal representation for union members","Workers who do not join the union being ostracised ('sent to Coventry')","A pay rise for all workers","The adrenaline rush of protesting"],a:1,e:"p. 116. A is a positive selective incentive, C is the collective good itself, D is a process benefit."}
