const OPEN_A=[
{t:"hayek",pts:12,q:"A Dutch municipality wants to meet its housing target by deciding every large development 'on its merits' through negotiated, discretionary deals, with the council approving the overall programme only as a package. (a) Explain how Hayek (1944, 'Planning and democracy') would criticise this approach, using at least three concepts from the chapter. (b) Contrast this with how a Marxist-inspired planner (as presented in HC4) would evaluate the same approach.",
model:"(a) Hayek would see a small-scale version of the mechanism in ch. 5. A housing programme serves many competing ends (affordability, density, green space, profit), and there is no 'complete ethical code' ranking them; the council can agree that there must be a plan but not on its content. Presenting it as a package leaves parliament (here the council) only to accept or reject the whole, so criticism 'cannot direct' (p. 51). Deciding cases 'on their merits' is exactly the delegation of discretion Hayek calls arbitrary power (p. 49); general rules, even local ones, would be acceptable. The officials negotiating deals must choose between ends and therefore impose their own scale of preferences (p. 48). What prevents arbitrariness is 'not the source but the limitation of power' (p. 53): democratic approval of the package does not make the discretion legitimate. Hayek's alternative is to restrict collective action to where true agreement exists and to use general rules. (b) A Marxist planner (Madden & Marcuse in HC4) would judge the approach on outcomes: markets turn housing into a commodity and produce material inequality, so the problem is not too much discretion but that deals with developers reproduce the structural logic of profit. They would support stronger, deliberate intervention (decommodified public or cooperative housing) and treat Hayek's formal equality of rights as blind to property power. Contrast: liberty and limited, rule-bound power vs material equality and structural transformation.",
rubric:["Uses at least three chapter concepts correctly (complete ethical code, delegation/discretion vs general rules, plebiscitarian/take-it-or-leave-it plan, limitation of power)","Applies them to the case rather than reciting","Marxist evaluation: structure, commodification, material equality, deliberate transformation","Explicit contrast of normative commitments (liberty/equality of rights vs material equality)"]},
{t:"needham",pts:12,q:"(a) Explain Needham's (2012) distinction between the private-law and public-law ways in which the state protects interests in land, and why the choice between them is partly political and partly technical. (b) Use Olson's logic of collective action (Hindmoor 2006) to explain the 'technical' part. (c) What does this imply for Needham's proposal to reach planning goals by creating markets in property rights?",
model:"(a) People have interests in how land is used, their own and others' (neighbours, traffic, clean air). The state protects some by making them property rights: the holder enforces them in court (private law). It protects others by empowering a public authority to restrict how owners exercise their rights, through permits or prohibitions (public law = land-use planning). Some interests are not protected. Whether an interest is protected is political (my interest in my neighbour's door colour is judged less important than hers). Which route is used is partly technical: a right of support works because cause and effect are clear and few people are involved; for air pollution affecting hundreds, a property right would be ineffective (p. 28). (b) The clean-air case is a collective action problem: winning a lawsuit against the factory is a non-excludable good for all residents, so each has an incentive to free-ride on others' legal action. It is a latent group with diffuse, small individual stakes, while the factory is a single concentrated actor. Unless there are selective incentives, a privileged member or low monitoring costs, nobody sues, so the state provides the good through public law (coercion). (c) Markets in property rights work only where interests can be organised: small estates with covenants and a residents' association (an intermediate group with monitoring), or one landlord who internalises the shared interest (a privileged actor). At regional scale, effects are too dispersed, which explains Needham's conclusion: feasible 'only under certain limited circumstances' (p. 34).",
rubric:["Correct private vs public law distinction with examples","Political (door colour) vs technical (right of support vs clean air) allocation","Correct Olson concepts (non-excludability, free-riding, latent group, possible solutions) applied to the case","Links back to Needham's limited-feasibility conclusion (e.g. residents' association / landlord vs regional scale)"]},
{t:"olson",pts:10,q:"In HC6, the slow regulation of short-term rentals (Airbnb) was explained as a collective action problem. (a) Explain, using Hindmoor's (2006) account of Olson, why neighbours affected by short-term rentals may fail to act collectively. (b) Discuss two mechanisms from the chapter through which they might still organise. (c) Give one limitation of the Olsonian explanation that Hindmoor himself identifies.",
model:"(a) Stricter regulation is a collective good for all neighbours: non-excludable, because every resident benefits from less noise and more housing supply whether or not they campaigned. Campaigning costs time and effort, and each individual's contribution makes little difference, so each prefers to free-ride. This is an N-person prisoners' dilemma: staying home is the dominant strategy, even though everyone campaigning is Pareto-superior. Many dispersed neighbours who hardly know each other form a latent group, while hosts or investors have concentrated stakes (lecture). Hindmoor notes that size is only 'an empirical tendency'; what matters is whether members can see who contributes. (b) Selective incentives: a residents' association (VvE) could offer members-only benefits such as legal advice (positive) or social pressure on non-participants (negative), though this raises a second-order problem that an entrepreneur (an active neighbour or local politician) may solve. Conditional cooperation in intermediate groups: in one building or street, long-term neighbours can monitor each other and cooperate tit-for-tat style, as Ostrom shows for commons. Process benefits (the satisfaction of protecting 'our' neighbourhood) or a privileged member who pays the whole cost are also valid. (c) Olson assumes self-interest: people also act because norms say they ought to, and the theory cannot explain how residents come to identify with a group or how collective action changes policy (pp. 122–128).",
rubric:["Collective good, non-excludability and free-riding correctly applied","Two mechanisms from the chapter, explained and applied (selective incentives, conditional cooperation, process benefits, privileged group, coercion)","Nuance: size only an empirical tendency / Olson does not say it is impossible","One limitation taken from Hindmoor (self-interest and norms; no theory of identity or group formation)"]}
];
